WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
 Hello, and welcome to another walkthrough for Computer Architecture for Everybody.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.680
 In the previous walkthrough, we went through a simple sort of WAT/WASM situation where we're

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:17.200
 talking to JavaScript. This is not really C code. It's hello world, but it's not C code. This is

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:24.240
 WASM plus being run in a JavaScript context, and the hello world is coming out of a console log

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:30.960
 function in JavaScript. I want to shift gears a little bit, focusing still again on hello world,

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:36.080
 and show you something for another one of my classes, C Programming for Everybody,

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:41.680
 under the write C option. So in this course, I have a C auto grader. Right now I'm just using

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.520
 the playground. You can write C programs and then compile them. So let's go ahead and write our C

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:52.400
 program. Now I'm going to hit the run code here, and you'll see a little spinner going on, and

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:57.200
 there's a bunch of work. And if you do the view source and you watch the network, I'm not going

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:06.880
 to show you the whole network. What you'll see is this C code is being sent up to the CC4E server

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:18.320
 using a piece of software called Emscripten, which compiles C code and other things into WASM. Then

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.800
 this environment will download the WASM, and I've got it watching for a string WASM in the

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.920
 network requests, and it will download that WASM, and then it will execute the WASM,

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.600
 and it will put the output here. I mean, I already executed it, but I made a change,

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:40.480
 hello world exclamation. So in the next click, it is going to compile it, compile to WASM

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:48.240
 inside the server, download the WASM back into the browser, and then execute the WASM in JavaScript.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.440
 So it's going to be sort of boring, it's just going to work. But watch for the spinner in the

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.880
 middle here. There goes, to see the spinner was there for just like a quarter of a second.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:05.920
 I ought to slow it down. But it did that, and there is this a.out WASM. And if we take a look

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:12.160
 at the response, yeah, it's not going to show it to us because it's kind of binary stuff, okay? So

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:18.560
 that was the WASM that came back. Now, that WASM executed with a JavaScript framework to start that

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:27.200
 WASM up, and then it printed hello world. Now, standard io.h is in C, printf is in the C standard

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:34.800
 library. So what has Inscriptium done? Well, what Inscriptium has done is it has compiled

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:42.160
 all of the standard libraries into WASM. It has compiled our code into WASM. It's linked it all

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:49.280
 together and sent down a really big WASM because this #include, if you know C, it just pulls a

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:56.880
 file in. And so that got compiled into WASM too. So let's take a look at how much WASM really came

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:04.080
 down. So let's take a look here. This is a dump of the WASM for hello world. And there's a lot here,

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:14.080
 but it's not that much, right? We got, let's see, 3C00 hex. I don't know how big that is,

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:19.120
 but I just scrolled through the whole thing. But this is not just my hello world code.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:25.680
 This is hello world plus some of the standard library and some of the standard io.h, all compiled

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:34.320
 into WASM. Now, this now is self-contained as WASM code ready to run inside of the browser. And

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:40.400
 that's of course what happened when it ran in the browser and out came hello world. But there's a

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.440
 lot to it because we're pulling in the C libraries and converting them to WASM as well. So this is

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:52.320
 kind of an ugly version of it. We can take a look at a little prettier version of it by looking at,

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:59.920
 not much prettier, we can look at the WAT. And so we're looking at the WAT here. This was written

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:05.360
 to be readable. This is the WAT code editor for the hello world. That's a pure WASM JavaScript

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:11.040
 example and you can kind of read it and you'll change it in your auto grader in a bit. This is

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:17.120
 more like what WASM does because it ran the in script and compiler and produced the WASM. And

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:23.120
 then I converted the WASM to WAT so that we could see it. And we're not going to change this. There's

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:28.560
 all kinds of stuff. FD write, FD close, FD seek. That's part of the standard library. And you see

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:36.640
 it's really like busy stuff. It's calling things and setting parameters and pushing them on the

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:41.760
 stack and calling them and making functions. And do we have any loops here? Let's see if we can get

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:50.400
 to a loop. There's a block. There's some blocks. There's some loops and jumps and all kinds of

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:59.520
 stuff. This is the output of a compiler that is writing to a pseudo machine language in WAT. So

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:09.840
 I'll just scroll through this. That is a lot of WAT. And it's not very easy to see. But

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:18.640
 it's pretty yummy and delicious to the runtime environment in JavaScript who has built an

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:25.440
 interpreter, much like the interpreter we built for the CDC 6504. And it runs that as the

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:31.760
 interpreter. So the key thing is that I'm not going to spend a lot of time having you kind

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:37.760
 of learn to code WAT. It's not intended for you to code it. It's intended for compilers to produce

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:43.680
 it. So just imagine that this C program that we're writing is really like a high performance,

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:51.760
 maybe a video game shading library or something that would get compiled into WASM, which would

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:58.240
 be super high performant. And then that WASM might be recompiled into raw machine language

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:04.080
 by my browser. And then it'd be like super fast in the browser. And that's the whole,

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:08.000
 that's literally the whole point of WASM. The WASM is to make it so you can write

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:14.560
 approaching machine code speed code in a portable, safe, sandboxed way,

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:20.480
 running in the browser under the rules of JavaScript. And inside that browser,

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:26.880
 you can mix code from many sources and many trust domains. And they can't harm each other

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:32.320
 because they're all running in individualized sandboxes. Okay. So I hope that was a bit

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:38.720
 interesting about how we connect C programming and WASM and how I use it in my C programming

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:48.560
 for everybody course. Cheers.

